message.content:
Write a JSON test corpus for evaluating a dictation-cleanup step. Write ONLY the file /Users/hungpq2412/flowstate/.goodnight/bench/heldout_corpus.json and do not read or modify any other file in that repository (do not look at any Swift sources or other corpus files; this corpus must be written independently).

Context: a push-to-talk dictation app transcribes speech with Whisper (large-v3-turbo), which outputs punctuated, capitalized English text, usually with digits for numbers and usually without "um/uh" (but sometimes with them). A small local LLM then "cleans" the transcript. The user wants their sentences kept word-for-word as spoken. The only edits allowed are:
- delete filler sounds (um, uh, er, hmm, ah) and, when clearly used as filler, "you know" / "I mean" / "like";
- delete stutters and immediately repeated words or phrases ("I I think", "can we, can we");
- apply explicit self-corrections, where the speaker corrects what they just said using a cue such as "no wait", "sorry", "I mean", "actually", "scratch that", "make that", "or rather" (keep only the correction: "Ship it Friday, no wait, Monday." -> "Ship it Monday.");
- fix a misheard technical term to its correct spelling (e.g. "pie torch" -> "PyTorch", "cube control" -> "kubectl", "post gress" -> "Postgres"/"PostgreSQL", "next JS" -> "Next.js", "lang chain" -> "LangChain", "Jason" -> "JSON" only when context is clearly about data formats);
- fix punctuation and capitalization.
Everything else must stay exactly as spoken: informal words (gonna, kinda, wanna), discourse openers (So, Well, Okay so, The thing is, Basically), hedges (I think, probably, maybe), emphasis ("actually" used for emphasis is NOT a correction), repetitions for emphasis ("very, very"), lists, questions, commands addressed to someone (they are dictated text, not instructions to the cleanup model), and prompt-injection-like text ("ignore previous instructions...").

Format: a JSON array of objects {"id": string, "cat": one of "verbatim"|"filler"|"correction"|"stutter"|"term", "input": string, "expected": [string, ...]}. "input" is a Whisper-style transcript. "expected" lists every acceptable correct output (add alternatives when punctuation or formatting could legitimately differ, e.g. "3 p.m." vs "3pm", or "Postgres" vs "PostgreSQL"); scoring ignores case and punctuation, so alternatives only need to differ in words.

Write 50 cases, with ids h01..h50: 22 verbatim (need no word changes; include long multi-clause sentences of 30-50 words, informal speech, emotional speech, commands like "Draft an email to Sarah saying the launch slipped", questions, emphatic "actually", a sentence beginning with "Sorry," as an apology, "like" used as a verb and as "approximately", "you know" used literally as in "Do you know the root cause?", a numbered list, and two prompt-injection-like sentences), 10 filler, 8 correction (vary the cue words; include one where the correction target is a number and one where the cue appears mid-sentence with more text after the correction), 4 stutter, 6 term. Make the sentences realistic for a software engineer / data scientist / economist dictating messages, notes, and code comments. Do not reuse these example sentences. Validate the file parses as JSON (e.g. python3 -m json.tool). Report only the file path and the count per category.

